<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Semey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Semipalatinsk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Semey, known until 2007 as Semipalatinsk, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> river in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, founded as a Russian fort in 1718. It was the seat of the Alash Orda government in 1917–1920 and a centre of Kazakh literary life associated with Abai Kunanbaev. From 1949 to 1989 the nearby Semipalatinsk Test Site was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s main nuclear weapons testing ground, with lasting effects on the region's health.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/semey.jpg" alt="Semey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexey Zhadanov · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2-minaret_mosque_in_Semey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Semey lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh River</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 1,000 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a> and 700 km south-east of the Russian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omsk">Omsk</a>. It has a cold semi-arid climate with warm summers and very cold winters, and low rainfall throughout the year. It stands on the Turkestan-Siberia Railway, which links it with Almaty, Barnaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novosibirsk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Russian settlement dates from 1718, when a fort was built beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> near the ruins of an ancient Buddhist monastery with seven buildings. The fort and later the city took the name Semipalatinsk, Russian for Seven-Chambered City, from the monastery. The fort suffered frequent flooding from snowmelt, and in 1778 it was moved 18 km upstream to higher ground. A small city grew around it and served the river trade between the nomadic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. On 19 May 1854 it became the capital of Semipalatinsk Oblast, and the Turkestan-Siberia Railway of the early 20th century made it a major point of transit between Central Asia and Siberia.</p><p>The city was one of the main centres for publishing books by the Kazakh intelligentsia. Between 1917 and 1920 it was the capital, under the name Alash-qala, of the largely unrecognised Alash Autonomy, set up after the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>. Red Army forces loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> took control in 1920. From October 1941 to July 1942 a Polish diplomatic post connected with the formation of Anders' Army stood in the city.</p><p>In 1949 the Soviet atomic bomb programme chose a site on the steppe 180 km west of the city for weapons testing. The Semipalatinsk Test Site operated until 1989, with 456 nuclear tests, of which 340 were underground and 116 atmospheric. Fallout and the uncontrolled exposure of workers led to high rates of cancer, childhood leukaemia and birth defects among residents of neighbouring villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 2007 the city council voted unanimously to change the name from Semipalatinsk to Semey, because the old name had negative associations with the atomic testing. Semey became the administrative centre of the new Abai Region in 2022. It is a university town, home to Semey Medical University. The city has a museum to the poet Abai Qunanbayuli, and a museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-dostoevsky">Fyodor Dostoevsky</a>, who is listed among its notable residents, which opened in 1971 and is the only one of seven Dostoevsky museums outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Semey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The USSR exploded its first atomic bomb at <b>Semipalatinsk</b> in Kazakhstan, years earlier than the West expected.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-29">29 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Alash Orda, led by Bukeikhanov, sought a Kazakh autonomy within a democratic Russian federation and governed from <b>Semey</b> (Semipalatinsk) with an eastern branch, while a western wing operated in Zhympity.</blockquote><a href="/state/alash-autonomy">Alash Autonomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviet nuclear test site near <b>Semipalatinsk</b> carried out over 450 tests from 1949 to 1989, and Baikonur launched Sputnik and Yuri Gagarin.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazakh-ssr">Kazakh SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state inherited the <b>Semipalatinsk</b> test site, which was closed, and a large nuclear arsenal, which it gave up by 1995.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></li></ul></section>
